      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name changed from Edwardsville to Drummond after a Northern Pacific engineer or local trapper Hugh Drummond.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drummond%2C_Montana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A one‑room schoolhouse museum, now run by the Chamber of Commerce, preserves the town’s early education history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://treasurestate.com/montana-towns/drummond/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Drummond’s landscape still feels like a classic Montana cow town, with working ranches and an unpretentious vibe.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://treasurestate.com/montana-towns/drummond/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The nearby Nimrod Hot Springs, 14.8 miles from Drummond, offers a historic mineral water spot tied to the region’s early travelers.",
